{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-153051","model_call":82,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"target forensic services laboratory\" shoplifting)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"target forensic services laboratory\" shoplifting)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_170985302#5_373374162","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1318","rank":1,"snippet":"a nonprofit independent from Target. Recode reached out to the SafeZone program in Minneapolis but did not hear back in time for publication. Over the years, Target has expanded its work with police nationwide. A 2010 report by the Police Executive Research Forum, a law enforcement membership organization, says that “Target and local partners have started Safe City projects in more than 20 other cities across the United States.” That’s not all. Target critics are also pointing to a 2011 article from Minnesota Public Radio (MPR) describing how the company created the <cover>Target Forensic Services Laboratory focused on investigating a range of crimes including shoplifting. </cover>The featured lab was located at Target’s Brooklyn Park campus in Minneapolis, and, according to MPR, it stayed busy “lending its high-end technology and professionals to law enforcement agencies free of charge.” The article points out that the Target lab carries the same certification used by any crime lab at a police department. “We ask for nothing in return.\" , \"start_char\": 3468.000000 , \"title\": \"Target looting draws attention to company’s history with police - Vox\" , \"url\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_170985302#5_373374162","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":235},"model_calls":82,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
